-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Replacement Policy (2Q)
+
+Cache entries are divided between two queues:
+
+- **Probationary queue (A1)** — entries admitted on first use.
+- **Protected queue (Am)** — entries that were requested again while cached.
+
+A newly rendered sheet enters A1. When a request finds the key already in A1, the entry is promoted to Am; a hit on an Am entry leaves it in Am. To stay within the 12 GiB bound, eviction draws from A1 first and from Am only when A1 is empty, so one-use sheets from a long sequential screening churn through A1 without displacing the smaller working set of comparison sheets held in Am.
+
+Queue membership and any replacement history are private bookkeeping owned by this worker. No other node reads them, pins entries, or depends on a particular clip remaining cached. The `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}` contract, the FIFO render queue with at most four active jobs, and the 12 GiB disk budget are unchanged by this policy.
+
+The size split between A1 and Am is a tunable parameter (see Open Questions).
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Preview cache eviction policy | 2Q: a probationary first-use queue (A1) and a protected reused queue (Am), with promotion on reuse | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Large sequential screenings should not evict the small set of comparison sheets that editors revisit. 2Q isolates one-use sheets in the probationary queue so reused sheets survive a scan, at the cost of two queues and a tunable size split. The extra bookkeeping is acceptable because it stays private to the worker and is small next to re-rendering a ~450 ms sheet. |
+
